/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd-184-Sun and Abraham (2021) point out a major limitation of event study regressions:  when there is **selective treatment timing** the $\mu_l$ end up being weighted averages of treatment effects *across different lengths of exposures*.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd-185-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd-186-**Selective treatment timing** means that individuals in different groups experience systematically different effects of participating in the treatment from individuals in other groups.  For example, there would be selective treatment timing if individuals choose to be treated in earlier periods if they tend to experience larger benefits from participating in the treatment.  This sort of selective treatment timing is likely to be present in many applications in economics / policy evaluation.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd-187-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:188:Contrary to event study regressions, pre-tests based on group-time average treatment effects (or based on group-time average treatment effects that are aggregated into an event study plot) **are still valid even in the presence of selective treatment timing**.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd-189-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd-190-To see this in action, let's keep the same example as before, but add selective treatment timing.
