612-*Note that the content of this section is only valid for `fixest` version 0.9.0 or later.*
613-
614-Despite TWFE's long-standing popularity for DiD designs, recent research has a shone a light on the biases that it can introduce under differential timing. For example, under staggered treatment or policy rollout. 
615-
616:For staggered DiD analyzes, `fixest` provides the `sunab` function for implementing the unbiased method of [Sun and Abraham (2020)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jeconom.2020.09.006) --- hereafter: SA20. The method is based on the estimation of `treated cohort x time to treatment` dummies which are later aggregated to obtain the average treatment for the treated (ATT) for each period (or for the full post treatment period).
617-
618-(Aside: Note that we use `time to treatment` rather than just `time`, because we need to account for the staggered treatment via a relative time measure.)
619-
620-Let's walk through an example with the `base_stagg` data set, where (a) different cohorts get the treatment at different periods, and (b) the effect of the treatment is cohort-specific and increasing over time. Let's have a first look at the data:
